    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Trigonometric Derivatives</title>

<p workspace="1in">
    The next class of functions that we want to find derivatives for are the trigonometric functions. What should the derivative of <m>\sin(x)</m> look like?
</p>

<image width="60%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="deriv-sin-graph">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/deriv-sin-graph.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>
<image width="60%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="deriv-sin-graph-tangents">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/deriv-sin-graph-tangents.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>
<image width="60%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="deriv-sin-graph-blank">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/deriv-sin-graph-blank.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>

<p component="b" workspace="2in">
    <nbsp />
</p>
<p component="a">
    So, maybe this should be cosine? That's about what it looks like. Let's see if we can get there from the definition.
</p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the derivative of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> from the definition of the derivative.
                </p>
            </statement>
        <solution component="a">
            <p>
                Involves the sin(x) limits from before.
            </p>
        </solution>
        </example>
        
    </page>
    <page>
        
        <p component="b" workspace="1in">
            So, our conclusion is that
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            So, our conclusion is that the derivative of <m>\sin(x)</m> is <m>\cos(x)</m>.
        </p>

        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            We can set up the same thing with cosine.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            We can set up the same thing with cosine. Don't show all of the work, but get to how the process works.
        </p>
    </page>
