    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Introduction to the Chain Rule</title>
        <example workspace="2in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Assume that you are on (or analyzing, if that's not your thing) a roller coaster, and you have two functions:
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>x(t)</m>, which is the position of the car on the track <m>t</m> seconds after the ride begins, and
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>h(x)</m>, the height of the roller coaster at each position <m>x</m> on the track (measured from the starting position)
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How would you determine your height <m>t</m> seconds after the start of the ride?
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What does <m>h'(t)</m> measure?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        How else could you compute <m>h'(t)</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>

        <p>
            The important take-away here is that when we want to combine rates of change, they often multiply. This is particularly relevant for compositions of functions, which we will see with the <em>Chain Rule</em> starting tomorrow.
        </p>
        
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
